"# Panel Size Complaints in Primary Care\n\nBased on the provided context, here are the main complaints related to panel size:\n\n## Panel Overload Issues\n\n• **Excessive patient loads**: Physicians report seeing 18-21 patients per day with some mentioning colleagues handling 25-30+ patients daily, leading to burnout and quality concerns [1i1ns95]\n\n• **Administrative burden**: Large panels create \"astronomical administrative burden\" with endless inbasket management, requiring 1.5-2 hours per day plus weekend catch-up work [1mrgzy4]\n\n• **Inherited problematic panels**: Doctors inheriting panels from retiring physicians often get \"affluenza/geriatric/entitled elderly patient base\" accustomed to concierge-level care and same-day responses [1mrgzy4]\n\n## Impact on Work-Life Balance\n\n• **No downtime**: Physicians report \"extremely little downtime\" with constant movement and no time for administrative tasks during clinic hours [1i1ns95]\n\n• **After-hours work**: Large panels force doctors to work evenings and weekends, with one physician noting they're \"sitting up now at 10PM still doing inbasket/notes\" [1i1ns95]\n\n• **Inability to close panels**: Employed physicians report frustration with \"not having the freedom to close my panel even though I'm booked solid through September\" [1jji9r3]\n\n## Quality of Care Concerns\n\n• **Complex patient management**: Even with \"reasonable patient load (max 18 patients per day, usually 15ish...but complex and old)\", physicians struggle to provide adequate care due to administrative demands [1mrgzy4]\n\n• **Pressure for productivity**: Corporate employers push for higher patient volumes regardless of complexity, with physicians feeling like \"indentured servants\" rather than healthcare providers [1mrgzy4]\n\n## Systemic Problems\n\n• **Inadequate support**: Large panels combined with insufficient support staff (only MAs, no RNs) make panel management unsustainable [1mrgzy4]\n\n• **Patient expectations**: Large panels often come with demanding patients who expect immediate responses and extensive services without understanding resource limitations [1mrgzy4]"